Master Prompt: McCormick SAP S/4HANA Coding Standards, Development Best Practices and Governance
Role and Objective

Act as a Senior SAP S/4HANA Technical Architect, ABAP Development Lead, ABAP Cloud Specialist, and Enterprise Development Governance Consultant.

Using the previously generated McCormick SAP S/4HANA Custom Development Naming Convention and Technical Standards document as the foundation, create a comprehensive organizational coding standards document for McCormick.

The new document must define how SAP custom developments are designed, coded, tested, secured, reviewed, deployed, and maintained.

The objective is to establish a consistent development approach across all relevant SAP S/4HANA business streams, including R2R, O2C, P2P, and shared enterprise developments.

The document must contain at least 150 individually numbered, actionable coding standards, organized into logical categories.

Do not simply list generic recommendations. Explain the standard, why it matters, when it applies, and how developers should implement it.

The document must be suitable for SAP developers, technical leads, solution architects, reviewers, and governing members.

1. Organizational Standards and Scope

Establish a clear hierarchy of standards:

Mandatory — must be followed unless an approved exception exists.
Recommended — preferred development practice.
Conditional — applicable when the relevant technology or business requirement is present.
Deprecated — should not be used for new development, with migration guidance where appropriate.

For each standard, specify its applicability and any relevant exceptions.

Distinguish between:

SAP technical requirements.
SAP-recommended development practices.
Proposed McCormick organizational standards.
Standards requiring confirmation from McCormick architecture, security, Basis, or governance teams.

Do not invent existing McCormick policies.

Use the naming convention document as the source of truth for object names. All code examples and repository object names must follow its underscore-separated naming convention wherever SAP technically permits it.

Use R2R, O2C, and P2P for examples. Shared objects must follow the previously defined stream-independent naming rules. CFL objects must not include a business-stream identifier.

